"Graph \\(\\ln(S/\\rho)\\) against \\(\\ln T\\) for protons in water and look at the residuals of the straight line fit. Work out the slope between {#} and {#} MeV from those two points and compare it with \\(1 - p\\) from Before the lab {#}. Over what energies is a single power law a poor description, and why?",
"Compare the recorded CSDA ranges with PSTAR at the same energies (in mm of water). Fit \\(\\ln R\\) against \\(\\ln T\\) for the PSTAR table from {#} to {#} MeV and give \\(\\alpha\\) and \\(p\\) with their uncertainties. Compare with Bortfeld’s values for water, \\(\\alpha\\) = {#} cm MeV⁻ᵖ and \\(p\\) = {#}.",
"At {#} MeV, compare the mass stopping powers of bone, lung, PMMA, aluminum and lead with water’s. Using the Bethe formula, explain the order from \\(Z/A\\) and \\(I\\) (the demo’s readouts give both). Why is the relative stopping power (per centimeter) of lead so different from its mass stopping power ratio?",
"Fill in the Bragg curves table. How does \\(R_{80}\\) compare with the CSDA range? How do the entrance dose and the width \\(R_{20} - R_{80}\\) change with energy? What does the No straggling peak depth tell you about where the end of range is compared with the peak?",
"Fill in the Range straggling table, with the uncertainty of each spread \\(\\sigma/\\sqrt{2(N-1)}\\) for \\(N\\) tracks. Graph \\(\\ln \\sigma\\) against \\(\\ln \\bar z\\) and find the slope and intercept. Compare with Bortfeld’s fit \\(\\sigma = 0.012 \\, \\bar z^{0.935}\\) (cm). Then compare \\(R_{20} - R_{80}\\) from Part {#} with \\(\\sigma\\) at the same energy and find their ratio.",
"Fill in the Water equivalent thickness table and compare the measured \\(t_w/t\\) with the relative stopping power at {#} MeV from Part {#}. Why might they differ slightly?",
"The detector depth is read to {#} cm, so each depth found by stepping it is uncertain by about half a step; on the distal edge the dose changes by several percent per {#} cm, so the dose you read at the depth nearest a level also tells you how far from the level you are. The spread of \\(N\\) simulated stopping depths has a statistical uncertainty of about \\(\\sigma/\\sqrt{2(N-1)}\\), and their mean one of \\(\\sigma/\\sqrt{N}\\); carry these into Analysis {#} and use the fit’s standard errors for the slope and intercept. The model itself differs from PSTAR by less than {#} mm in range from {#} to {#} MeV; compare that with your other uncertainties.",
"Turn nuclear reactions off and on at {#} MeV. What happens to the entrance dose compared with the peak, and why does the effect grow with energy?",
"Choose Carbon {#} MeV/u. Its range is close to that of {#} MeV protons. Why is its peak so much narrower? What does a real carbon beam show past its peak that this model leaves out?",
"ICRU Report {#} recommends \\(I\\) = {#} eV for water instead of PSTAR’s {#} eV. From the Bethe formula, estimate by how much the stopping power at {#} MeV and the range would change.",
"Choose Lung in the way. PSTAR’s lung tissue has a density of {#} g/cm³, but an inflated lung has about a quarter of that. How would the peak move for a real lung, and why does an error in the lung density in a treatment plan matter more for protons than for X rays?",
"Treatment plans often allow {#} % of the range plus {#} mm for range uncertainty. For a tumor whose distal edge is {#} cm deep, how many millimeters is that, and how does it compare with the straggling width you measured?",
"Choose Lead range shifter and turn on Stretch the tracks sideways. Why does lead widen the beam so much more than the same water equivalent thickness of plastic would?",
